# Phase I Cache Capacity Measurement Process

This README summarizes the process used to perform the Phase I
timing-only cache capacity experiments.

## 1. Compile the pointer-chasing benchmark

First, clone the repository onto the SSH server and enter the project
directory.

Compile the benchmark using:

gcc -O0 -g -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -fno-omit-frame-pointer main.c -lm -o cache_bench


## 2. Pin the benchmark to one CPU

Use:

lscpu -e=CPU,CORE,SOCKET,NODE

to view the CPU, core, socket, and NUMA-node mapping.

The benchmark should be pinned to one CPU while measurements are running.

For example:

taskset -c 4 ./cache_bench <arguments>


## 3. Run a coarse capacity sweep

Start with a large range of power-of-two working-set sizes.

For Sunbird, we tested from 4 KiB through 64 MiB:

taskset -c 4 ./cache_bench sweep 4096 67108864 1000000 64 random 12345 capacity_coarse

The important parameters are:

1000000 = timed samples per working-set size
64      = dependent pointer accesses per timed sample
random  = randomized pointer traversal
12345   = fixed random seed


## 4. Plot the coarse sweep and identify possible transitions

Plot the median latency against working-set size and look for regions
where latency begins to change significantly.

For Sunbird, the coarse sweep showed possible transitions around:

32–64 KiB
256–512 KiB
16–32 MiB

These are timing-derived candidate regions and should not yet be treated
as confirmed cache sizes.


## 5. Take dense measurements around the transition regions

After identifying possible transitions, test additional working-set
sizes within those regions.

For example, for the first Sunbird transition:

for size in 36864 40960 45056 49152 53248 57344 61440
do
    taskset -c 4 ./cache_bench single $size 1000000 64 random 12345 dense_$size
done

Repeat this process for the other transition regions identified from
the coarse sweep.


## 6. Copy the measurement files from the SSH server

From the local Windows machine, use SCP to copy the result files:

scp "<unityID>@<ssh-server>.ece.ncsu.edu:~/<project-directory>/dense_*_summary.csv" .

The period at the end tells SCP to place the files in the current local
directory.


## 7. Generate the main capacity graph

Use the Python plotting program to combine:

capacity_coarse_summary.csv
dense_*_summary.csv

Plot:

X-axis: Working-set size
Y-axis: Median latency (TSC ticks/access)

This graph combines the coarse sweep with the dense measurements and
makes the candidate capacity transitions easier to examine.


## 8. Generate box plots around the transitions

The median alone does not show the complete latency distribution.

Use the raw million-sample CSV files to create box plots for
representative working-set sizes below, near, and above each candidate
transition.

For Sunbird, three box plots were created corresponding to the three
main transition regions.


## 9. Test randomized vs. sequential traversal

Run sequential traversal at representative working-set sizes to test
whether regular memory access changes the observed latency behavior.

For example:

taskset -c 4 ./cache_bench single 49152 1000000 64 sequential 12345 prefetch_48k

Repeat at representative points from the other transition regions.


## 10. Plot randomized vs. sequential results

Compare the median latency from randomized pointer chasing with the
sequential measurements.

This comparison helps determine whether regular/sequential traversal is
being accelerated by mechanisms such as hardware prefetching and helps
justify using randomized dependent pointer chasing for capacity
inference.


## 11. Record the Phase I timing-only inference

Use the coarse sweep, dense measurements, box plots, and
randomized-vs-sequential experiment together to identify the
timing-derived candidate cache-capacity boundaries.

At this stage, do not use PMU counters or known/vendor cache
specifications to change the timing-only inference.

Save and commit the Phase I results before beginning Phase II
validation.
